# __PACKAGE_NAME__

GENERATED npm package candidate — do not hand-edit. Regenerate via `python3 compiler/tools/regen_all.py` in the DeliberateCode/System2 repo.

System2 is a deliberate-reasoning workflow for the [Pi coding agent](https://github.com/earendil-works/pi). This candidate contains the compiler-generated base workflow, prompts, and bounded safety extension. It has no install scripts or bundled runtime dependencies; Pi provides the declared host peer.

The package uses the Pi installation supplied by the user and does not enforce an exact host version. CI acceptance is reproducibly pinned to **Pi 0.85.1**. It covers package command discovery, Orchestrator-first public RPC/SDK startup, branch-local workflow restoration, visible human gate-decision audit messages, explicit gate skipping, repeatable full/maintenance workflow cycles with archived decision/evidence history, human status, subprocess contracts for all 13 roles, and a real Pi JSON-mode child executing the terminating completion-claim tool through a deterministic test provider. It also covers recoverable rejected claims, incomplete exits, safe failure categories, extension-derived partial-change inventories, the three-failure infrastructure circuit, bounded dangerous-command/sensitive-path blocks, structured off-scope writes, and benign negative controls. Hermetic subprocess doubles exercise failure and all-role invocation paths without external model traffic; they are not native CLI acceptance. This is evidence for the CI-tested version, not a compatibility claim for untested Pi versions.

The `"*"` peer range follows Pi's package guidance for host-provided core imports; it is a loader convention, not a claim of validation against every Pi version. Local acceptance runs against the installed Pi version so maintainers can test additional versions without changing System2.

A cohesive study-derived acceptance also reproduces the original `tasker` journey: full Gate 0–5 progression, all four specification artifacts, executor-owned `.gitignore`, implementation-child, test-engineer, and parent-Bash verification of 29 tests, post-execution reviews, visible gate decisions, and a root-level Markdown follow-up in a new maintenance workflow. It runs through real Pi 0.85.1 JSON agent loops with an offline deterministic provider and requires no manual repair or pasted test output. This proves the integration path without external traffic; it is not a claim that every live model will make equally good decisions. A supported live-model TUI repetition remains a pre-publication release check.

## Availability

This package is **pending publication**. There is no supported npm installation command, and the package name must not be treated as currently installable.

## Materialize project files

After publication, the package-discovered `/system2-init` command will materialize `.pi/SYSTEM.md` and `system2.pi.lock.json` into the current project, then reload Pi so resources are rediscovered. It does not materialize or replace caller-owned `AGENTS.md`. Re-running is idempotent: byte-identical managed files are skipped, locally modified managed files are left untouched unless `--force` is supplied, lexical/symlink escapes are refused, writes use atomic replacement, and unmanaged files are never touched.

## Scope

The candidate contains the **base 13-role workflow** composed with an empty overlay set. The parent remains the Orchestrator. Gate approval/rejection creates a visible transcript audit record of the human TUI action, normalized paths, and artifact evidence. `system2_request_gate_skip` requires an explicit interactive human decision, records each skipped/rejected gate, and cannot skip authority-establishing Gate 0. Gate 5 completes one workflow rather than the Pi session: the next request can start a full workflow, start a small maintenance workflow, or remain in completed read-only review mode. `/system2-new [full|maintenance]` starts a cycle directly and `/system2-history` shows archived gate decisions/evidence; new cycles start at Gate 0 without inherited authority or filesystem rollback. `system2_delegate` starts an ephemeral Pi subprocess with role-specific prompt/tools and no recursive delegation tool, then returns one bounded report. `system2_status` gives the parent a sanitized model-readable operational view while `/system2-status` remains human-facing. Parent Bash is available for independent verification under the dangerous-command and sensitive-path gates; each structured write/edit or supported explicit shell-write target requires interactive human approval for that one normalized operation. The parent supplies only role, objective, optional ordinary repository `contextInputs`, concrete expected outputs, acceptance criteria, verification commands, and optional budget/timeout; System2 derives gate authority, approval ID, and exact task scope. Non-empty child verification commands are accepted only for roles whose canonical tool projection includes Bash; other roles use `[]` and the parent verifies their artifacts independently. Bash-capable children must run each contracted command verbatim in a separate Bash call; the child completion tool rejects completion until every command has been observed, allowing the child to correct the omission in-process. An impossible role/verification combination is rejected before spawning. Context inputs need no gate-artifact approval: System2 normalizes and launch-snapshots them separately from byte-for-byte human-approved gate evidence, and children may discover other non-sensitive repository files normally. Human gate approvals use readable `allowedPaths` entries such as `spec/**`, `src/*.py`, and `.gitignore`, not regexes. Absolute paths are accepted only when they resolve inside the project and are normalized to repository-relative paths. Gate 4 requires concrete `plannedOutputs`: System2 rejects uncovered or role-impossible paths before approval and shows the human a path-to-compatible-role matrix. Delegation repeats the preflight and recommends compatible roles before spawning. The executor covers common task-approved project configuration such as `.gitignore`; docs-release covers task-approved Markdown in project-owned paths. `/delegate` is only a human convenience request to the parent; it never switches parent authority. Process, Pi-session, and model-context isolation are provided; filesystem and inherited-credential isolation are not. CLI sessions reuse Pi's resolved absolute entry; SDK hosts must set `SYSTEM2_PI_BIN` to an absolute owner-trusted, non-group/world-writable Pi executable. There is no bare `pi` `PATH` fallback. Ambient extensions are disabled in children, so models registered only by a custom-provider extension are unavailable and delegation fails closed.

Overlays and profiles are not end-user package UX. Applying them requires the source compiler; the published package will contain no compiler and will read no overlay input at install time or runtime.

## Enforcement fidelity

The package-discovered Pi extension registers `tool_call` handlers that block declared dangerous-command patterns, sensitive structured paths/ordinary literal shell tokens, structured off-lease writes, and supported off-lease shell redirection/`tee` targets before execution. Parent Bash supports verification and inspection; parent structured writes and supported explicit shell-write targets are one-operation, interactive-human-approved overrides recorded in the session with an exact request digest. Non-interactive parent writes fail closed. Implicit writes by tests/build tools and unsupported shell forms are not comprehensively classified. A child target must match its canonical role scope, the latest human-approved restricted path globs, the Gate 4 planned-output list when present, and the exact scope System2 derives from concrete expected outputs. `.pi/**` and `system2.pi.lock.json` remain protected even under a broad approved glob. Lease enforcement is adapted/partial, not a general shell-write parser: commands such as `touch`, `cp`, `mv`, `install`, `sed -i`, interpreters, and build tools are unsupported. Dangerous-command matching does not claim arbitrary-shell normalization, and sensitive-token matching does not expand shell expressions. Malformed or overflowing supported extraction fails closed; unknown custom tool schemas are outside the sensitive-path claim.

Budget enforcement is adapted/partial: System2 takes bounded before/after task-scope snapshots, derives changed paths and artifact hashes, records contracted Bash outcomes from Pi events, and computes a conservative prefix/suffix changed-line count for bounded text files. The changed-line budget counts additions plus removals, so rewrites and test updates require headroom; callers should retain the large default when no meaningful bound is known. Only directories that can intersect the approved task scope are traversed, including dependency/build directories when approved; `.git` remains excluded. It is not a native VCS diff; incomplete, binary, oversized, or symbolic-link changed-file evidence cannot produce a complete outcome, and unsupported out-of-scope shell writes are outside both the task-scope snapshot and lease claim. System2 quiesces the child process group/tree and blocks common explicit detachment forms before after-state capture, but arbitrary daemon/session escape is not proven. Child models submit only outcome/summary/blockers/risks/follow-ups; rejected claims can be corrected up to three times. Missing claims return `incomplete`, while runtime failures expose a safe category, diagnostic digest, and observed partial-change inventory without raw stderr. Failures are classified as infrastructure, authorization, application, contract, or operator; only application failures consume the corrective/boomerang budget. A missing contracted verification event is `VERIFICATION_NOT_RUN` in the contract domain, does not increment the infrastructure streak, and directs the parent to verify independently or correct the role-aware contract. Three consecutive genuine infrastructure failures pause delegation behind a branch-local circuit until the human reviews `system2_status` and runs `/system2-resume`; repeating `/system2-resume` after closure is an explanatory no-op. Failed/incomplete delegations are never rolled back automatically: changed paths remain in place for preview, and rollback requires explicit human approval for exact operations. Formatting and type-checking remain advisory. The model cannot supply gate IDs, approval IDs, regex task scopes, gate-evidence lists, or digests. Optional context inputs are launch-snapshotted repository data rather than human-approved evidence; System2 separately derives and verifies approved gate-evidence bytes at dispatch. Completed workflows block delegation and mutation until the human starts a new full or maintenance cycle. Invalid/interrupted workflow state blocks mutation and delegation until explicit human recovery; an extension upgrade changes the provenance digest and therefore also requires recovery of an in-progress workflow. See `system2.pi.lock.json` for the per-capability report.

